					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/croquembouche" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2893766); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=2893766&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1607522045" alt="Croquembouche" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=2893766)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/croquembouche" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2893766); return false;" >Croquembouche</a></span> <span class="odate time_1607518064 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">09 Dec 2020 12:47</span>
																	<br/>
						in discussion <a href="/forum/c-50742/per-page-discussions">Per Page Discussions / Per page discussions</a> &raquo;
						<a href="/scp-6666-j/comments/show">SCP-6666-J</a>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4840535">
									

<p>There's something really interesting happening here. The containment procedures and description have this perfect balance of fleshed-out detail and conciseness. It implies an article that isn't forthcoming; it sets up an expectation that despite the OOC context of the topic and despite the J in the title, this is going to be a serious piece. I think I forgot that I was reading a -J. There's nothing funny about the top half of the article, in my opinion, it is very much how I feel the Foundation would respond to this absurd scenario, and what could have been handled immaturely is handled with grace - without so much as a &quot;(sic)&quot; - respecting the reader enough to assume that they are reading maturely.</p>
<p>Then the interview happens, and it's the kick that sets off the dominoes. We know exactly what happens and there's a chain of events hidden behind the almost childish &quot;ow!&quot;. There's a subtle but brilliant contrast between that and the article, but what gets me most is that it's all in the reader's head - the article is never explicitly funny, it never stops treating the reader like an adult. I was not expecting to find a laugh so far through such a short article.</p>
<p>I feel like I've seen this tried before, but never as well as it's done here. Even outside the context of the -J, the article makes sense, everything about it is justified. There's nothing here that doesn't serve a purpose, with even apparently-extraneous detail setting up the juxtaposition.</p>
<p>+1</p>

							</div>

								
					
